The global `crimes` binary isn’t on this runner’s PATH, so the exact command failed before scanning. I found the fixture source and am checking for the repository-built CLI to run the equivalent context command against the same file.

The context scan confirms the three requested findings. It also reports `boolean_naming_drift` on the negative flags, which is a symptom of the same negative-flag problem.

1. `option_bag_junk_drawer` — `describeCheckout`

Replace the generic `options` parameter with a purpose-specific type and destructure its fields:

```ts
interface CheckoutDescription {
  plan: string;
  role: string;
  region: string;
  currency: string;
  status: string;
  retry: boolean;
}

export function describeCheckout({
  plan,
  role,
  region,
  currency,
  status,
  retry,
}: CheckoutDescription): string {
  return [plan, role, region, currency, status, String(retry)].join(":");
}
```

Why: today an agent may treat `CheckoutOptions` as a general checkout configuration bag, add unrelated properties, or miss that all six fields participate in the serialized output. The narrower name, destructuring, and explicit return type make the function’s exact contract visible.

2. `return_shape_roulette` — `resolvePaymentResult`

Define an explicit discriminated union:

```ts
type PaymentResult =
  | { kind: "error"; code: string; message: string }
  | { kind: "redirect"; url: string; permanent: boolean }
  | { kind: "success"; value: string; cached: boolean };

export function resolvePaymentResult(/* input */): PaymentResult {
  if (input.error) {
    return {
      kind: "error",
      code: input.code ?? "unknown",
      message: input.message ?? "Payment failed",
    };
  }

  if (input.redirect) {
    return {
      kind: "redirect",
      url: input.url ?? "/",
      permanent: false,
    };
  }

  return {
    kind: "success",
    value: input.value ?? "",
    cached: false,
  };
}
```

Ideally, make the input a discriminated union too. Today the three return objects have as little as 0% key overlap. An agent might access `result.ok`, `result.url`, or `result.value` without narrowing, add a fourth ad-hoc shape, or accidentally return missing data because every input field is optional.

3. `negative_flag_maze` — `canRunBilling`

Invert the API to positive, named flags:

```ts
interface BillingRunPolicy {
  isBillingEnabled: boolean;
  shouldRetry: boolean;
}

export function canRunBilling({
  isBillingEnabled,
  shouldRetry,
}: BillingRunPolicy): boolean {
  return isBillingEnabled && shouldRetry;
}
```

Why: `!disableBilling && !skipRetry` requires two mental inversions. An agent adding another condition could invert one incorrectly, and two positional booleans are easy to swap at a call site. Positive properties make both the predicate and calls readable.

There are no in-repo callers or basename-matched tests for these functions, so the refactor should add focused tests for every payment-result variant and all four billing-flag combinations.
